    // ===================== UNIT 4b: FORMS OF DISCOURSE =====================
    {
      id: `M1U7`,
      title: `Forms of Discourse, Modes of Reading and Usage`,
      objectives: [
        `Identify narrative, descriptive, expository and argumentative/persuasive discourse and their purposes and features.`,
        `Use the form of an extract as evidence when stating the writer's purpose.`,
        `Distinguish efferent, aesthetic and critical modes of listening and reading.`,
        `Recognise idiomatic expressions, colloquialisms, argot, clichés, jargon and slang, and judge when each is appropriate.`
      ],
      videos: [
        {title:`The four forms of discourse`, q:`four modes of discourse narration description exposition argumentation explained`},
        {title:`Efferent and aesthetic reading`, q:`efferent and aesthetic reading Rosenblatt explained`},
        {title:`Clichés, jargon and slang`, q:`difference between cliche jargon slang colloquialism examples`}
      ],
      screens: [
        {
          type:'learn', title:`Four forms of discourse`,
          html:`<p><b>Discourse</b> is any extended piece of spoken or written language. The syllabus groups genres into broad forms. Each form has a typical <b>purpose</b> and recognisable <b>features</b>.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<tr><th>Form</th><th>Typical purpose</th><th>Features</th><th>Genres</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Narrative</b></td><td>to entertain, to share an experience, to reflect</td><td>a sequence of events in time; characters; setting; conflict; often first or third person; past tense; dialogue</td><td>short stories, novels, anecdotes, journal entries, autobiographies</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Descriptive</b></td><td>to help the reader see, hear and feel a person, place or moment</td><td>sensory imagery; precise adjectives and verbs; figurative language; spatial order (left to right, near to far)</td><td>travel writing, character sketches, poetry, parts of stories and features</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Expository</b></td><td>to inform, explain, instruct</td><td>facts, definitions, examples; logical order (process, cause and effect, classification); neutral, objective tone; headings</td><td>explanations, instructions, directions, reports, news articles, informative blogs, vlogs, vodcasts and podcasts</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Argumentative / persuasive</b></td><td>to convince, persuade, criticize, call to action</td><td>a clear position or claim; reasons and evidence; rhetorical appeals (logos, ethos, pathos, kairos); counter-argument and rebuttal; emotive language; call to action</td><td>opinion pieces, editorials, advertisements, speeches, persuasive blogs, vlogs and podcasts</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p><b>Argumentative vs persuasive:</b> argumentative writing relies mainly on <b>reasoning and evidence</b> and acknowledges the other side; persuasive writing leans more on <b>emotion and the writer's credibility</b> to move the reader to act. Most real texts blend the two.</p>
<p>Notice that digital genres such as blogs, vlogs, vodcasts and podcasts can be <b>expository or persuasive</b>: a cooking vlog that shows how to make johnnycakes is expository; a vlog urging viewers to boycott plastic straws is persuasive.</p>
<p class="tip"><b>Exam tip:</b> The Paper 2 extract for Module 1 is usually expository or argumentative. Naming the form in your introduction ("This argumentative feature article...") gives your purpose statement a solid foundation.</p>`,
          tutor:[
            {h:`Four questions`, p:`<p>Does it tell a story? <b>Narrative</b>. Does it paint a picture? <b>Descriptive</b>. Does it explain or instruct? <b>Expository</b>. Does it try to change my mind? <b>Argumentative/persuasive</b>.</p>`},
            {h:`Look at the verbs`, p:`<p>Story verbs in the past ("she ran") → narrative. Sense words ("glittering", "salty") → descriptive. "First, then" or "is defined as" → expository. "Must", "should", "we cannot allow" → persuasive.</p>`}
          ],
          plain: "<p>There are four main forms of writing. <b>Narrative</b> tells a story with events and characters. <b>Descriptive</b> paints a picture using the senses. <b>Expository</b> explains or informs with facts and steps. <b>Argumentative or persuasive</b> takes a side and tries to convince you. A recipe for johnnycakes is expository, a blog urging readers to give up plastic straws is persuasive, and a story about a ferry trip to Saba is narrative.</p>",
          deeper: "<p>The difference between argumentative and persuasive writing is one of <b>emphasis</b>. Argument leads with <b>logos</b>: claims, evidence and a fair treatment of the opposing view, followed by a rebuttal. Persuasion leans on <b>pathos</b> and <b>ethos</b>, aiming to move the reader to act. A newspaper editorial in Trinidad on a property tax might be argumentative, while a campaign advertisement on the same issue is persuasive. Do not be misled by the medium: a podcast or vlog can belong to any form, so judge by features, not format. In your Paper 2 introduction, naming the form precisely (\"this argumentative feature article\") shows the examiner that you have read the extract as a whole before analysing its parts.</p>"
        },
        {
          type:'sort', game:true, title:`Which form of discourse?`,
          intro:`<p>Each excerpt comes from a text. Sort it by its main form of discourse.</p>`,
          main:`Narrative, descriptive, expository or argumentative/persuasive?`,
          bins:[`Narrative`,`Descriptive`,`Expository`,`Argumentative / persuasive`],
          items:[
            {t:`"Her market stall was a riot of colour: scarlet peppers, golden mangoes and bunches of green thyme piled high."`, bin:1, fb:`Vivid sensory detail to help the reader see the stall: descriptive.`},
            {t:`"To register for the scholarship, first download the form, then attach your transcript, and finally submit it before 30 June."`, bin:2, fb:`Step-by-step instructions with sequence words: expository.`},
            {t:`"The old salt pond on Grand Turk lay flat and silver under the noon sun, its edges crusted white and smelling faintly of sea and mud."`, bin:1, fb:`Sight, touch and smell images with no argument and no story: descriptive.`},
            {t:`"The morning I sat my first CAPE exam, I woke at four, prayed, ate nothing, and walked the long way to school."`, bin:0, fb:`A personal sequence of past events: a narrative journal-style account.`},
            {t:`"Sargassum is a brown seaweed that drifts in large mats across the Atlantic and can release hydrogen sulphide as it rots."`, bin:2, fb:`A definition and explanation in a neutral tone: expository.`},
            {t:`"If we truly love this island, we must ban single-use plastics now, before our reefs are lost for good."`, bin:3, fb:`A clear position, "must", an emotional appeal and urgency: persuasive.`},
            {t:`"When the ferry finally reached Saba, Keisha grabbed her bag and ran up the pier, terrified she had missed her grandmother's birthday."`, bin:0, fb:`Events in sequence, a character and rising tension: narrative.`},
            {t:`"Although some argue that a curfew protects teenagers, the evidence suggests that youth programmes reduce crime more effectively."`, bin:3, fb:`A claim, an opposing view and evidence-based reasoning: argumentative.`}
          ],
          win:`Excellent. You identified each form from its features: sequence and characters, sensory detail, explanation, or a position supported by reasons and appeals.`,
          tutor:[
            {h:`Story or picture?`, p:`<p>Events happening one after another → narrative. A scene described with the senses → descriptive.</p>`},
            {h:`Explain or convince?`, p:`<p>Facts and steps in a neutral tone → expository. "Must", "should", "although some argue" → argumentative/persuasive.</p>`}
          ],
          easy:{
            title:`Which form of discourse? (Foundation)`,
            items:[
              {t:`"Keisha grabbed her bag and ran up the pier, terrified she was late."`, bin:0, fb:`Events and a character: narrative.`},
              {t:`"The salt pond lay flat and silver, its edges crusted white."`, bin:1, fb:`Sensory detail: descriptive.`},
              {t:`"First download the form, then attach your transcript."`, bin:2, fb:`Instructions: expository.`},
              {t:`"We must ban single-use plastics now!"`, bin:3, fb:`A position and a call to action: persuasive.`}
            ]
          },
          up: {"title":"Which form of discourse? (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>These excerpts blend features: a narrative may contain description, and an argument may contain facts. Sort each one by its <b>dominant</b> form.</p>","items":[{"t":"\"In 1902, when Mount Pelée erupted, the ash cloud swallowed Saint-Pierre in minutes; my great-grandfather, fishing offshore, watched the town vanish.\"","bin":0,"fb":"A past event in sequence, seen through a character: narrative, even though it contains vivid detail."},{"t":"\"After the shop closed, Marcus counted the day's takings twice, sighed, and switched off the light.\"","bin":0,"fb":"A character and a sequence of actions: narrative."},{"t":"\"The rum shop smelled of sawdust and lime, and dominoes cracked on the tables like small gunshots.\"","bin":1,"fb":"Smell, sound and a simile, with no story or argument: descriptive."},{"t":"\"Each Easter, the kite flyers on Georgetown's seawall turn the sky into a moving quilt of red, yellow and blue.\"","bin":1,"fb":"A visual image and a metaphor (\"moving quilt\") help the reader see the scene: descriptive."},{"t":"\"Unlike hurricanes, which form over warm water and can be tracked for days, earthquakes strike without warning and cannot be forecast.\"","bin":2,"fb":"A neutral comparison that explains a difference: expository."},{"t":"\"Coral bleaching occurs when stressed corals expel the algae that give them their colour and much of their food.\"","bin":2,"fb":"A definition and explanation in an objective tone: expository."},{"t":"\"To make cassava bread, first grate the peeled cassava, then squeeze out the juice, and finally bake the flour on a hot griddle.\"","bin":2,"fb":"Process steps with sequence words: expository."},{"t":"\"Critics say a sugar tax punishes the poor; yet studies in Barbados found that sales of sugary drinks fell after the tax was introduced. The tax should stay.\"","bin":3,"fb":"A claim, an opposing view and evidence used to rebut it: argumentative."},{"t":"\"Imagine your grandmother's house under three feet of water. Now imagine it happening every year. Vote yes for the sea wall.\"","bin":3,"fb":"An emotional appeal followed by a call to action: persuasive."}],"win":"Excellent. You looked past individual features to the form that controls each excerpt."}
        },
        {
          type:'learn', title:`From form to purpose: reading the clues`,
          html:`<p>Identifying the form is not the end of the analysis. It is <b>evidence for the writer's purpose</b>. Each form suggests a family of purpose verbs:</p>
<ul>
<li><b>Narrative</b> → entertain, share, reflect, and sometimes warn or criticize through a story's lesson.</li>
<li><b>Descriptive</b> → evoke, celebrate, highlight, help the reader experience.</li>
<li><b>Expository</b> → inform, explain, educate, instruct, compare.</li>
<li><b>Argumentative / persuasive</b> → persuade, convince, criticize, warn, encourage.</li>
</ul>
<p><b>Mixed forms are normal.</b> Many feature articles open with a short <b>narrative</b> hook (an anecdote about one family), add <b>description</b> to make the scene vivid, use <b>exposition</b> to give facts, and end with an <b>argument</b> and a call to action. In that case the <b>dominant</b> form, the one that controls the whole extract, tells you the main purpose. The other forms are <b>strategies</b> serving it.</p>
<p><b>Worked example:</b> An article begins with Mr. Richardson watching the sea flood his Belize City home (narrative), describes the brown water and ruined furniture (descriptive), explains how storm surge forms (expository) and ends: "Government must fund sea defences before the next season" (argumentative).</p>
<p class="passage"><b>Purpose statement:</b> The writer's purpose is to <b>persuade</b> the government to fund sea defences, using a narrative opening and vivid description to make readers feel the human cost, and exposition to explain the danger.</p>
<p class="tip"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> In your reflective journal, note one extract where you first misjudged the form because of its opening. What made you change your mind?</p>`,
          tutor:[
            {h:`Form points to purpose`, p:`<p>Story → entertain or reflect. Picture → evoke. Explain → inform. Argue → persuade or criticize.</p>`},
            {h:`Find the boss form`, p:`<p>If the text mixes forms, ask which one the whole extract is building towards. That is the dominant form and the main purpose.</p>`}
          ],
          plain: "<p>Once you know the form, you have a clue to the purpose. Stories usually entertain or share an experience; description helps you see and feel; exposition informs or explains; argument persuades. Many articles mix forms: a story to start, some description, some facts, then an argument. The form that controls the whole piece, the <b>dominant</b> form, tells you the main purpose. The others are tools that help it.</p>",
          deeper: "<p>Openings mislead. A feature that begins with a fisherman in Gouyave watching his catch shrink looks narrative, but if it ends with \"The Ministry must act\", every earlier part was serving an argument, so read the ending before you decide. Then explain the <b>function</b> of each subordinate form: the anecdote creates <b>pathos</b> and makes a statistic human, the description makes the danger vivid, and the exposition adds <b>logos</b>. That analysis is worth far more than a list of forms. Watch, too, for criticism without argument: a story about a long hospital queue can criticise the health service without a single argumentative sentence, by letting the events speak for themselves.</p>"
        },
        {
          type:'learn', title:`P.I.E.: writing to persuade, inform or entertain`,
          html:`<p>Writers always have a purpose. Most writing falls under one of three headings, which you can remember as <b>P.I.E.</b></p>
<div class="trio">
<div class="tcard"><span class="lbl">P · Persuade</span><p>Convince; challenge opposing ideas; coax the reader into accepting a view.</p></div>
<div class="tcard"><span class="lbl">I · Inform</span><p>Educate; present facts, processes and data. Also called <b>expository</b> writing.</p></div>
<div class="tcard"><span class="lbl">E · Entertain</span><p>Engage emotion and imagination; tell a story; appeal to the five senses.</p></div>
</div>
<h4>Writing to inform (expository writing)</h4>
<p>Informative writing explains a function, describes a process, presents facts or ideas, or simply shares information. It is primarily factual. <b>Examples:</b> academic textbooks (biology, accounts, PE), statistical reports, incident reports, biographies and autobiographies, news stories.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<tr><th>Organisational strategy</th><th>How it appears in informative writing</th></tr>
<tr><td>Sequence</td><td>Moves the reader through time: first, secondly, next, in conclusion. Common in history and social studies.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Process</td><td>The steps for carrying out an activity (recipes, experiments). Related to sequence, but focused on the activity rather than time.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Cause and effect</td><td>Presents factual relationships rather than arguments (it also appears in persuasion).</td></tr>
<tr><td>Compare and contrast</td><td>Appears in both persuasive and informative writing: look at the surrounding features to decide which purpose it serves.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Classification</td><td>Grouping things into categories, as in the classification of living things in biology.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Problem and solution</td><td>A problem and its resolution: common in mathematics and science texts.</td></tr>
</table></div>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<tr><th>Language technique</th><th>What to look for</th></tr>
<tr><td>Historical data</td><td>References to past events, dates and contexts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Statistical data</td><td>Figures, percentages, ratios and numerical findings presented objectively.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Factual data from credible sources</td><td>Data from reliable websites or publications. Not all online information is factual: always verify the source.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sequence transition words</td><td>first of all, in the beginning, secondly, consequently, in the first place.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Academic jargon</td><td>Subject-specific vocabulary (medical, legal, scientific) signalling an informative, academic context.</td></tr>
</table></div>
<h4>Writing to entertain</h4>
<p>Entertaining writing presents vivid details that appeal to the five senses, uses language creatively, evokes emotion through imagery and tells a story. <b>Examples:</b> short stories, novels and novellas; poetry, spoken word and slam poetry; fables, folklore and myths; plays and skits; realistic fiction and film scripts; journals, diaries and blogs; narrative essays; songs; biographies written with entertaining language.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<tr><th>Organisational strategy</th><th>Signal</th></tr>
<tr><td>Chronological order</td><td>Events from first to last: beginning, middle and end.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Conflict, action and resolution</td><td>A problem is introduced, action verbs drive the narrative and a resolution is reached.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Characters and dialogue</td><td>Named characters speaking to each other confirm a narrative or dramatic text.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Narrative point of view</td><td>First person (I, we) or third person (he, she, they): who is telling the story.</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p><b>Language techniques that signal entertainment:</b> figurative language (metaphor, simile, personification, onomatopoeia, hyperbole), which rarely appears in informative text; imagery ("the deep reds of this inferno"); sense data (sight, sound, smell, taste, touch); action words; descriptive adjectives; and transition words, which are assessed at both CSEC and CAPE level.</p>
<h4>P.I.E. quick reference</h4>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<tr><th>Text type</th><th>Most likely purpose</th><th>Text type</th><th>Most likely purpose</th></tr>
<tr><td>Poetry</td><td>Entertain (sometimes persuade)</td><td>Historical writing</td><td>Inform</td></tr>
<tr><td>Eulogy</td><td>Inform (sometimes entertain)</td><td>Debates</td><td>Persuade</td></tr>
<tr><td>Songs</td><td>Entertain</td><td>Research</td><td>Inform</td></tr>
<tr><td>Advertisements</td><td>Persuade</td><td>Plays</td><td>Entertain</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p class="tip"><b>Exam tip:</b> At the bottom of your Module 1 Paper 2 passage there is a <b>source citation</b>. Read it first: it tells you where the information came from and helps you predict the tone and purpose before you read the body. Some texts blend more than one purpose, so identify the <b>dominant</b> one and support it with evidence from the text.</p>`,
          tutor:[
            {h:`P, I or E?`, p:`<p>Is the writer trying to change my mind (P), teach me something (I) or make me feel and imagine (E)?</p>`},
            {h:`Clues for inform`, p:`<p>Statistics, dates, jargon, sequence words, an objective voice.</p>`},
            {h:`Clues for entertain`, p:`<p>Characters, dialogue, imagery, the five senses, figurative language.</p>`}
          ],
          plain: "<p>Most writing aims to <b>Persuade</b>, <b>Inform</b> or <b>Entertain</b>: P.I.E. Persuasive writing tries to change your mind, like an advertisement or a debate speech. Informative writing gives facts, steps and data, like a textbook or a news report. Entertaining writing tells stories and uses vivid, sensory language, like an Anansi story or a calypso. Read the source line under the passage first, because it hints at the purpose.</p>",
          deeper: "<p>P.I.E. is a starting point, not a final answer. In an essay, upgrade the broad label to a precise verb: a news report on rising rents in Bridgetown may <b>inform</b>, but closer reading may show that it <b>highlights</b> the hardship of young families. Strategies such as compare and contrast or cause and effect appear in both informative and persuasive writing, so look at the surrounding features (neutral or emotive diction, the presence of a call to action) to decide which purpose they serve. Watch for texts that disguise persuasion as information: sponsored content or a company \"report\" may use statistics and a neutral tone while its real aim is to sell. Spotting that disguise is critical reading at its best.</p>"
        },
        {
          type:'sort', game:true, title:`P, I or E?`,
          intro:`<p>Sort each text type by its most likely main purpose.</p>`,
          main:`What is the most likely purpose of this text?`,
          bins:[`Persuade`,`Inform`,`Entertain`],
          items:[
            {t:`A school debate on banning phones in class`, bin:0, fb:`Debates exist to persuade the audience and judges.`},
            {t:`A radio advertisement for a new phone plan`, bin:0, fb:`Advertisements aim to persuade you to buy.`},
            {t:`A calypso performed at the Carnival Village`, bin:2, fb:`Songs are mainly written to entertain.`},
            {t:`A police incident report on a minibus collision`, bin:1, fb:`Incident reports record facts: inform.`},
            {t:`A candidate's campaign speech in Basseterre`, bin:0, fb:`Campaign speeches aim to win votes: persuade.`},
            {t:`A biology textbook chapter on photosynthesis`, bin:1, fb:`Academic textbooks explain and educate: inform.`},
            {t:`A one-act play staged by the drama club`, bin:2, fb:`Plays tell stories through characters and dialogue: entertain.`},
            {t:`An Anansi story told at a storytelling evening`, bin:2, fb:`Folklore engages imagination and emotion: entertain.`},
            {t:`A statistical report on tourist arrivals at V. C. Bird International Airport`, bin:1, fb:`Statistical reports present data objectively: inform.`}
          ],
          win:`Well sorted. Remember to name the dominant purpose and prove it with evidence from the text.`,
          tutor:[
            {h:`Ask what the writer wants`, p:`<p>Action or agreement = persuade. Knowledge = inform. Enjoyment and feeling = entertain.</p>`}
          ],
          easy:{
            title:`P, I or E? (Foundation)`,
            items:[
              {t:`A radio advertisement for a new phone plan`, bin:0, fb:`Advertisements persuade.`},
              {t:`A statistical report on tourist arrivals`, bin:1, fb:`Reports inform.`},
              {t:`An Anansi story`, bin:2, fb:`Folklore entertains.`}
            ]
          },
          up: {"title":"P, I or E? (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Context matters more than text type. A song can persuade and a \"report\" can advertise. Sort each text by its most likely MAIN purpose in the situation described.</p>","items":[{"t":"A \"guest satisfaction report\" on a resort's own booking page showing that 98% of guests gave five stars","bin":0,"fb":"It looks like data, but it is published by the seller to win bookings: persuasion disguised as information."},{"t":"A bank's sponsored article titled \"Why every teenager needs a savings account with us\"","bin":0,"fb":"Sponsored content that names its own product aims to persuade."},{"t":"A political party's song played at a campaign rally","bin":0,"fb":"Songs usually entertain, but at a rally the aim is to win votes."},{"t":"A health poster urging Grenadians to \"Know your numbers: get your blood pressure checked\"","bin":0,"fb":"An imperative call to action: persuade."},{"t":"A Ministry of Health table of dengue cases by parish in Jamaica","bin":1,"fb":"Objective data presented for the public: inform."},{"t":"A news report on the opening of a new terminal at V. C. Bird International Airport","bin":1,"fb":"A factual account of an event: inform."},{"t":"A biography of Sir Garfield Sobers that sets out his career dates and statistics","bin":1,"fb":"Biographies focused on facts and dates mainly inform."},{"t":"A spoken word poem about a grandmother's kitchen performed at a literary festival","bin":2,"fb":"Imagery and emotion for an audience's enjoyment: entertain."},{"t":"A comic skit about a minibus driver performed at a school concert","bin":2,"fb":"Characters, humour and performance: entertain."},{"t":"A folk tale about Papa Bois told to children at bedtime","bin":2,"fb":"Folklore engages the imagination: entertain."}],"win":"Sharp reading. You judged purpose by context and features, not by the label on the text."}
        },
        {
          type:'mcq', title:`Form and purpose in an extract`,
          passage:`<blockquote class="passage"><p>Last August, fifteen-year-old Dario cycled to Willemstad every morning, a hot, dusty ride along a road with no shoulder and trucks roaring past within an arm's length.</p><p>Road safety data from many countries show that separated cycle lanes reduce injuries. Painted lines cost little; a protective kerb costs more but saves lives.</p><p>Our island cannot keep asking children to gamble with their lives to get to summer jobs. The Ministry must build a protected cycle lane on this route before next summer.</p></blockquote>`,
          q:`Which statement BEST identifies the dominant form of this extract and the writer's purpose?`,
          options:[
            {t:`It is mainly narrative; the writer's purpose is to entertain readers with Dario's story.`, fb:`The extract opens with a narrative hook, but Dario's story is a strategy, not the goal. The final paragraph shows what the whole extract is building towards.`},
            {t:`It is mainly expository; the writer's purpose is to explain how cycle lanes are built.`, fb:`Paragraph 2 is expository, but the extract does not explain construction; it uses facts to support an argument.`},
            {t:`It is mainly descriptive; the writer's purpose is to describe the road to Willemstad.`, fb:`The description of the road makes the danger vivid, but it serves the argument rather than being the point of the extract.`},
            {t:`It is mainly argumentative/persuasive; the writer's purpose is to persuade the Ministry to build a protected cycle lane, using a narrative hook and expository evidence to support the claim.`, ok:true, fb:`Correct. The dominant form is argumentative: a clear claim ("must build") supported by an anecdote, description and evidence. You also named how the other forms serve the purpose.`}
          ],
          tutor:[
            {h:`Read the ending`, p:`<p>"The Ministry must build..." is a claim and a call to action. That signals argumentative/persuasive.</p>`},
            {h:`The other forms help`, p:`<p>The story of Dario (narrative), the trucks roaring past (descriptive) and the road safety data (expository) all support the argument.</p>`}
          ],
          easy:{
            title:`Form and purpose (Foundation)`,
            options:[
              {t:`Mainly narrative, to entertain readers with Dario's story.`, fb:`The story is only the hook. The ending shows the real aim: a demand for action.`},
              {t:`Mainly argumentative, to persuade the Ministry to build a protected cycle lane.`, ok:true, fb:`Yes. The final paragraph makes a claim and calls for action; the story and facts support it.`}
            ]
          },
          up: {"title":"Form and purpose in an extract (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","passage":"<blockquote class=\"passage\"><p>When the first lionfish was spotted off Little Cayman in 2008, divers photographed it as a curiosity.</p><p>Native to the Indo-Pacific, lionfish have no natural predators in the Caribbean. A single female can release around two million eggs a year, and the fish eat young snapper and grouper that would otherwise replenish the reefs.</p><p>Several territories now run culling programmes, and some restaurants serve lionfish to encourage fishing. Scientists continue to study whether these measures can keep populations under control.</p></blockquote>","q":"This extract opens with a story and mentions restaurants. Which statement BEST identifies its dominant form and purpose?","options":[{"t":"Mainly argumentative; the purpose is to criticise governments for failing to stop the lionfish.","fb":"Nothing in the extract blames anyone. The tone stays neutral throughout."},{"t":"Mainly argumentative; the purpose is to persuade readers to eat lionfish.","fb":"Restaurants are mentioned as a fact, not urged on the reader. There is no claim, no \"should\" and no call to action."},{"t":"Mainly expository; the purpose is to explain how lionfish spread through the Caribbean and how territories are responding, with a brief narrative opening to engage readers.","ok":true,"fb":"Correct. The facts, the cause-and-effect explanation and the neutral ending (\"Scientists continue to study\") show an informative purpose. The opening anecdote is only a hook."},{"t":"Mainly narrative; the purpose is to tell the story of the first lionfish sighting.","fb":"The story lasts one sentence. The rest of the extract explains causes and responses."}]}
        },
        {
          type:'pick', game:true, title:`Find the argument`,
          intro:`<p>This blog post mixes several forms of discourse.</p>`,
          q:`Tap a sentence that is argumentative or persuasive (it states a position or urges action). Three sentences qualify; the feedback names them all.`,
          lines:[
            `The old wooden schoolhouse in Grand Bay still stands at the top of the hill, its green paint peeling in the sun.`,
            `My grandmother walked there every morning in the 1950s, carrying her slate and a piece of bread.`,
            `Heritage buildings are structures recognised as having historical or cultural importance.`,
            `We cannot allow this treasure to be bulldozed for yet another car park.`,
            `The council should restore the schoolhouse and open it as a museum of island education.`,
            `Sign the petition today and share this post with everyone who loves our history.`
          ],
          correct:[3,4,5],
          win:`Well done. Sentence 1 is descriptive, sentence 2 narrative and sentence 3 expository. Sentences 4–6 state a position and urge action, so the post's dominant form is persuasive.`,
          miss:`Look for sentences that take a side ("cannot allow", "should") or tell the reader to act ("Sign", "share"). Description, story and definition are supporting forms.`,
          tutor:[
            {h:`Signal words`, p:`<p>"Cannot allow", "should", "must", and commands like "sign" or "share" signal persuasion.</p>`},
            {h:`The others`, p:`<p>Peeling green paint = description. Grandmother's walk = narrative. A definition = exposition.</p>`}
          ],
          easy:{
            title:`Find the argument (Foundation)`,
            q:`Tap the ONE sentence that is persuasive.`,
            lines:[
              `The old schoolhouse stands at the top of the hill, its green paint peeling.`,
              `The council should restore the schoolhouse and open it as a museum.`,
              `Heritage buildings are structures of historical importance.`
            ],
            correct:[1],
            win:`Yes. "Should restore" takes a position and urges action.`,
            miss:`Look for the sentence that tells someone what they should do.`
          },
          up: {"title":"Find the argument (Challenge)","bloom":"Analyse","intro":"<p>This blog post about Fort Shirley at the Cabrits, Dominica, mixes forms. Some persuasion is disguised as a question or a proposal, and one sentence offers evidence without arguing.</p>","q":"Tap EVERY sentence that is argumentative or persuasive (it takes a position, proposes action or tries to sway the reader).","lines":["The ruins of Fort Shirley stand above Prince Rupert Bay on the Cabrits peninsula.","The fort was built by the British in the eighteenth century.","Last month I watched a tour group climb the steps, read one sign and leave within ten minutes.","Is a single faded sign really the best we can offer visitors to one of our finest heritage sites?","Interactive guides and trained local storytellers would turn a ten-minute stop into a morning's experience.","Heritage tourism is tourism that focuses on historic sites and cultural traditions.","Studies of heritage tourism suggest that visitors who stay longer at a site spend more at nearby businesses.","It is time the Ministry treated the Cabrits as a classroom, not a car park for cruise buses."],"correct":[3,4,7],"win":"Well done. Sentence 4 is a rhetorical question that pushes the reader to say \"no\", sentence 5 proposes a solution, and sentence 8 is the claim and call to action. Sentences 1 and 2 are descriptive and expository, sentence 3 is narrative, sentence 6 is a definition, and sentence 7 is expository evidence that supports the argument without arguing itself.","miss":"Look again. Persuasion is not only \"should\" and \"must\": rhetorical questions and proposals also sway the reader. But evidence on its own (\"studies suggest...\") is expository, even when it supports an argument."}
        },
        {
          type:'learn', title:`Efferent, aesthetic and critical modes`,
          html:`<p>The syllabus describes three <b>modes</b> of listening and reading. The mode depends on your purpose, and an exam extract often needs all three.</p>
<div class="trio">
<div class="tcard"><span class="lbl">Efferent</span><p>Listening or reading to <b>take information away</b>: facts, instructions, main points. You focus on informative statements. Example: noting the evacuation routes in a hurricane bulletin.</p></div>
<div class="tcard"><span class="lbl">Aesthetic</span><p>Listening or reading for the <b>experience</b>: enjoying and responding to expressive, emotional language, rhythm and imagery. Example: being moved by a calypso about a lost friend, or a monologue read aloud.</p></div>
<div class="tcard"><span class="lbl">Critical</span><p>Listening or reading to <b>identify and evaluate</b> a writer's purpose, techniques and ideas: Is the argument sound? Is the source credible? What is the speaker trying to make me believe?</p></div>
</div>
<p><b>In Paper 1, Section A</b>, an extract is read aloud to you. You may need efferent listening for the main idea, aesthetic listening for tone and imagery, and critical listening to identify devices and the purpose.</p>
<p><b>In Paper 2</b>, you read efferently to grasp the content, then critically to analyse how language and organisation serve the purpose.</p>
<p class="tip"><b>Exam tip:</b> If a question asks how a line makes you <i>feel</i> or what tone it creates, switch to aesthetic mode. If it asks whether a claim is <i>reliable</i>, switch to critical mode.</p>`,
          tutor:[
            {h:`Three reasons to read`, p:`<p><b>Efferent</b> = to get information. <b>Aesthetic</b> = to enjoy and feel. <b>Critical</b> = to judge and evaluate.</p>`},
            {h:`Same text, different modes`, p:`<p>You can read one speech for facts, for its emotional power and to judge its argument, one after another.</p>`}
          ],
          plain: "<p>There are three ways to read or listen. <b>Efferent</b>: to take information away, like noting the evacuation routes in a hurricane bulletin. <b>Aesthetic</b>: to enjoy and feel the language, like being moved by a calypso. <b>Critical</b>: to judge the writer's purpose, techniques and ideas, asking whether you should believe them. In an exam you often need all three, one after the other.</p>",
          deeper: "<p>The modes are not separate boxes; skilled readers move between them on purpose. When you hear the Paper 1 listening extract, listen efferently for the main idea, then aesthetically for tone and imagery, and finally critically for purpose and devices. The aesthetic response feeds the critical one: if a line moves you, ask <b>how</b> the writer achieved that effect. A common misconception is that critical reading means finding fault. It means evaluating, which includes recognising strengths. Election speeches in St. Lucia, for example, reward critical listening: the rhythm may sweep you along aesthetically, while critical listening checks whether each promise is supported by evidence.</p>"
        },
        {type:'checkin', title:`Quick check-in`},
        {
          type:'learn', title:`Usage: idioms, colloquialisms, argot, clichés, jargon and slang`,
          html:`<p>Expression marks reward <b>appropriate usage</b>: choosing words that suit the audience, purpose and level of formality. The syllabus names these usage categories:</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>
<tr><th>Usage</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th><th>In formal writing?</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Idiomatic expression</b></td><td>a phrase with a figurative meaning that cannot be worked out from the words</td><td>"not my cup of tea"; Caribbean: "cut your eye", "hard ears"</td><td>Use sparingly and only where the meaning is clear.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Colloquialism</b></td><td>informal, conversational wording</td><td>"kids", "a lot of", "get on someone's nerves"</td><td>Replace: "children", "many", "irritate".</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Argot</b></td><td>the secret or private vocabulary of a particular group, often to keep outsiders out</td><td>coded words used by a gang or by gamers in a clan chat</td><td>Avoid, except when quoting.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Cliché</b></td><td>an overused expression that has lost its force</td><td>"at the end of the day", "in this day and age", "think outside the box"</td><td>Avoid; replace with a precise, fresh phrase.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Jargon</b></td><td>specialised vocabulary of a profession or activity</td><td>"storm surge", "berth", "LBW"</td><td>Fine for experts; define it for a general audience.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Slang</b></td><td>very informal, often short-lived in-group words</td><td>"bro", "dawg", "no cap"</td><td>Avoid in formal writing.</td></tr>
</table></div>
<p><b>Pronunciation and enunciation</b> belong to expression too. <b>Pronunciation</b> is how a word is said (where the stress falls, which sounds are used); <b>enunciation</b> is how clearly you articulate each sound. In oral presentations, clear enunciation of key words helps a mixed audience follow you.</p>
<p class="tip"><b>Teacher's tip:</b> Keep a "cliché swap" list in your personal glossary: "in this day and age" → "today"; "at the end of the day" → "ultimately". Check your essays against it before you hand them in.</p>`,
          tutor:[
            {h:`Who uses it?`, p:`<p><b>Jargon</b> = professionals. <b>Argot</b> = a closed group keeping outsiders out. <b>Slang</b> = casual in-group talk. <b>Colloquialism</b> = everyday conversation.</p>`},
            {h:`Tired phrases`, p:`<p>A <b>cliché</b> is a phrase used so often it has lost its punch, like "at the end of the day".</p>`}
          ],
          plain: "<p>Good writers choose words that suit the audience. An <b>idiom</b> has a hidden meaning (\"hard ears\" means stubborn). A <b>colloquialism</b> is chatty wording (\"kids\"). <b>Argot</b> is a group's secret code. A <b>cliché</b> is a tired phrase (\"at the end of the day\"). <b>Jargon</b> is specialist language (\"storm surge\"). <b>Slang</b> is very informal (\"no cap\"). In formal essays, avoid clichés and slang, and explain any jargon.</p>",
          deeper: "<p>Usage is a tool for analysis as well as a rule for your own writing. When a columnist deliberately uses a Caribbean idiom such as \"cut your eye\", ask why: it may build <b>rapport</b> with local readers or signal shared identity, a point that links to Module 2. Jargon in an extract can build <b>ethos</b> (the writer sounds expert) or shut out a general audience; decide which it does. A cliché in an extract may weaken the writer's credibility, and in your own essay it costs Expression marks. One subtle distinction: argot is designed to <b>exclude</b> outsiders, while jargon is simply technical. Nurses who say \"triage\" are not hiding their meaning; a gang using code words is.</p>"
        },
        {
          type:'multi', game:true, title:`Name the usage`,
          intro:`<p>Identify the usage category of the underlined wording in each situation.</p>`,
          items:[
            {text:`A student writes in a formal report: "<b>In this day and age</b>, technology is everywhere."`, options:[`jargon`,`cliché`,`argot`], answer:1, fb:`An overused phrase that has lost its force: a cliché. Write "Today" instead.`},
            {text:`A Met Office officer tells a press conference that the "<b>storm surge</b>" could reach two metres.`, options:[`slang`,`jargon`,`colloquialism`], answer:1, fb:`Specialised vocabulary of meteorology: jargon. For the public, it should be explained.`},
            {text:`A group of online gamers invents coded words so that parents reading the chat cannot understand them.`, options:[`cliché`,`idiomatic expression`,`argot`], answer:2, fb:`A private vocabulary designed to exclude outsiders is argot.`},
            {text:`In an essay a student writes: "The council's plan really <b>gets on residents' nerves</b>."`, options:[`colloquialism`,`argot`,`jargon`], answer:0, fb:`Everyday conversational wording: a colloquialism. In formal writing: "irritates residents".`},
            {text:`Granny says the boy has "<b>hard ears</b>" because he never listens.`, options:[`jargon`,`cliché`,`idiomatic expression`], answer:2, fb:`A figurative phrase whose meaning (stubborn, disobedient) cannot be worked out from the words: an idiomatic expression.`}
          ],
          win:`Well done. Knowing these categories helps you analyse a writer's word choice and polish your own Expression marks.`,
          tutor:[
            {h:`Ask who and how`, p:`<p>A profession's words → jargon. A secret group's words → argot. A tired phrase → cliché. Casual talk → colloquialism. A figurative saying → idiom.</p>`}
          ],
          easy:{
            title:`Name the usage (Foundation)`,
            items:[
              {text:`"In this day and age" in a formal report.`, options:[`cliché`,`jargon`], answer:0, fb:`An overused phrase: cliché.`},
              {text:`A forecaster speaks of "storm surge".`, options:[`jargon`,`slang`], answer:0, fb:`Specialised vocabulary: jargon.`},
              {text:`Granny says he has "hard ears".`, options:[`idiomatic expression`,`argot`], answer:0, fb:`A figurative saying: idiom.`}
            ]
          },
          up: {"title":"Name the usage (Challenge)","bloom":"Evaluate","intro":"<p>Harder items: the categories overlap, and some questions ask whether the usage is appropriate for its audience.</p>","items":[{"text":"A cardiologist tells a medical conference that the patient had \"atrial fibrillation\". For this audience, the jargon is...","options":["argot, because outsiders cannot understand it","inappropriate, because jargon is always wrong","appropriate, because the audience shares the specialised vocabulary","a cliché"],"answer":2,"fb":"Jargon suits expert audiences. It only becomes a problem when the audience does not share it."},{"text":"Prisoners develop words so that guards cannot follow their conversations. This is...","options":["argot","jargon","slang","colloquialism"],"answer":0,"fb":"A private vocabulary designed to exclude outsiders is argot."},{"text":"A student's essay ends: \"Only time will tell whether the policy works.\"","options":["argot","jargon","cliché","idiomatic expression"],"answer":2,"fb":"An overused phrase that adds nothing: a cliché. Replace it with a specific judgement."},{"text":"A Guyanese teacher says the new student is \"still finding his feet\".","options":["slang","jargon","idiomatic expression","argot"],"answer":2,"fb":"The meaning (settling in) cannot be worked out from the literal words: an idiom."},{"text":"A Form 6 student writes to the principal: \"The new timetable is a total mess and loads of students are fed up.\" What is the usage problem?","options":["jargon","there is no problem; it is formal","colloquialisms that should be replaced with formal wording","argot"],"answer":2,"fb":"\"Total mess\", \"loads of\" and \"fed up\" are conversational. Try: \"The new timetable has caused confusion, and many students are dissatisfied.\""},{"text":"A gamer tells a friend that his teammate is \"cracked\" (extremely skilled).","options":["idiomatic expression","slang","cliché","jargon"],"answer":1,"fb":"Short-lived, informal in-group wording: slang. It is not designed to exclude outsiders, so it is not argot."},{"text":"Which revision BEST removes the cliché from \"At the end of the day, the tax will hurt small vendors\"?","options":["The tax, no cap, will hurt small vendors.","Ultimately, the tax will hurt small vendors.","When all is said and done, the tax will hurt small vendors.","At the end of the day, the tax will really hurt small vendors."],"answer":1,"fb":"\"Ultimately\" is precise and formal. The other options keep a cliché or add slang."}],"win":"Excellent. You can classify usage and judge whether it suits the audience, which is exactly what Expression marks reward."}
        },
        {
          type:'write', title:`One topic, two forms`, genre:`Descriptive and persuasive paragraphs with commentary`,
          prompt:`<p>Choose ONE place on your island that matters to you (a beach, a market, a hill, a schoolyard). Write:</p><ol><li>a <b>descriptive paragraph</b> (about 80–100 words) that helps a reader see, hear and feel the place, using at least two sensory images and one figurative technique;</li><li>a <b>persuasive paragraph</b> (about 80–100 words) addressed to the Minister of Tourism, arguing for ONE action to protect or improve the place, with a clear position, a reason, and a call to action;</li><li>a <b>short commentary</b> (2–3 sentences) naming the form of each paragraph and explaining how the change of form changed your purpose and word choice.</li></ol>`,
          min:180, max:320,
          frame:`<p><b>Descriptive:</b> At dawn, ... (sight) ... (sound) ... (smell/touch) ... (simile/metaphor) ...</p><p><b>Persuasive:</b> Minister, ... must ... because ... Therefore, I urge you to ...</p><p><b>Commentary:</b> My first paragraph is descriptive; its purpose is to ... My second is persuasive; its purpose is to ... The change of form led me to ...</p>`,
          checklist:[
            `My descriptive paragraph uses at least two senses and one figurative technique.`,
            `My persuasive paragraph states a clear position, gives a reason and ends with a call to action.`,
            `My commentary names both forms and links each to a precise purpose.`,
            `I avoided clichés and slang.`,
            `I checked my Standard English.`
          ],
          rubric:[
            {criterion:`Content`, marks:10, look:[`\\b(smell|scent|salt|sound|hear|glitter|shimmer|warm|cool|rough|sweet|roar|whisper)`, `\\blike\\b|\\bas a\\b|metaphor|simile`, `\\b(must|should|urge|I ask|call on)\\b`, `because|since|as a result`, `descriptive`, `persuasive|argumentative`, `purpose`], min:5,
              strong:`Both paragraphs show the features of their form, and your commentary links form to purpose precisely. Next step: make the call to action even more specific (who, what, by when).`,
              weak:`Make the forms distinct. The descriptive paragraph needs sensory images ("the salt-heavy breeze") and a figurative technique; the persuasive one needs a position ("The Ministry must..."), a reason ("because...") and a call to action. Then name each form and its purpose in your commentary.`},
            {criterion:`Organization`, marks:7, look:[`minister`, `therefore|consequently|for this reason|in conclusion`, `\\n`], min:2,
              strong:`Three clearly separated parts, each with a logical internal order. Your persuasive paragraph moves neatly from position to reason to action.`,
              weak:`Separate the three parts and give each a clear order: description in spatial or sensory order; persuasion as position → reason → action ("Therefore, I urge you to...").`},
            {criterion:`Expression`, marks:8, look:[`[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]`, `\\b(vivid|precise|urgent|evoke|highlight)`], min:1,
              strong:`Precise word choice suited to each form, with no clichés. Well controlled Standard English.`,
              weak:`Choose fresher, more precise words ("the sea glittered like broken glass" rather than "the sea was very nice") and remove clichés such as "at the end of the day". Proofread for tense and agreement.`}
          ],
          model:`<p><b>Descriptive paragraph</b></p><p>At dawn, Little Bay is a bowl of silence. The water lies flat and pale green, so clear that the ribs of sand beneath it look close enough to touch. A pelican drops like a stone and bursts up again with a silver fish flapping in its beak. The air smells of salt and wet seagrape leaves, and the sand, still cool from the night, squeaks under bare feet. Behind me, the first bus groans up the hill, but down here the bay keeps its own slow, early-morning time.</p><p><b>Persuasive paragraph</b></p><p>Minister, Little Bay must be protected from the litter that now spoils it every weekend. Overflowing bins and plastic bottles drift into the water, harming the fish and seabirds that make the bay worth visiting. Because residents and tourists alike depend on clean beaches, this is both an environmental and an economic issue. Therefore, I urge you to install covered bins and a weekly clean-up crew before the next cruise season begins.</p><p><b>Commentary</b></p><p>My first paragraph is descriptive; its purpose is to help the reader experience the bay through sight, sound, smell and touch, so I chose sensory images and a simile. My second paragraph is persuasive; its purpose is to persuade the Minister to act, so I shifted to a clear position, a reason linked to the economy, and a direct call to action in a formal register.</p>`,
          teacherTip:`<p>Changing form changes everything: purpose, audience, register and word choice. In Paper 2, when you name the form of an extract, immediately ask what that form is designed to do; this gives you a precise purpose verb. Avoid clichés in both forms; examiners reward fresh, precise language under Expression.</p>`,
          easy: {"title":"One topic, two forms (Foundation)","bloom":"Apply","genre":"Descriptive and persuasive paragraphs, scaffolded (25 marks: Content 10, Organization 7, Expression 8)","prompt":"<p>Choose ONE place on your island that matters to you. Write three short parts, using the frame.</p><ol type=\"a\"><li>A <b>descriptive paragraph</b> (about 50–70 words). Use TWO senses (what you see, hear, smell or feel) and ONE simile (a comparison using \"like\" or \"as\").</li><li>A <b>persuasive paragraph</b> (about 50–70 words) to the Minister of Tourism. Say what should be done, give ONE reason with \"because\", and end by asking the Minister to act.</li><li>ONE sentence saying which paragraph is descriptive and which is persuasive.</li></ol>","min":100,"max":200,"frame":"<p><b>(a) Descriptive:</b> At sunrise, ... is ... I can see ... I can hear ... The air smells of ... The sea is like ...</p><p><b>(b) Persuasive:</b> Minister, you should ... because ... Therefore, please ...</p><p><b>(c)</b> My first paragraph is descriptive because ... My second paragraph is persuasive because ...</p>","checklist":["My descriptive paragraph uses two senses and one simile.","My persuasive paragraph says what should be done, gives a reason with \"because\" and asks the Minister to act.","I wrote one sentence naming both forms.","I used Standard English and no slang."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Content","marks":10,"look":["smell|salt|hear|whisper|bright|calm|warm|cool|sound|see","\\blike\\b|\\bas a\\b","should|must|urge|please","because|since","descriptive","persuasive"],"min":4,"strong":"Both paragraphs show the features of their form.","weak":"Add two senses and a simile to the first paragraph, and a \"should\", a \"because\" and a request to the second."},{"criterion":"Organization","marks":7,"look":["minister","therefore|so|that is why","first|second"],"min":2,"strong":"Three clear parts in a sensible order.","weak":"Keep the three parts separate, and end the persuasive paragraph with \"Therefore, please...\""},{"criterion":"Expression","marks":8,"look":["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]"],"min":1,"strong":"Clear sentences in Standard English.","weak":"Start each sentence with a capital letter, end it with a full stop, and avoid slang."}],"model":"<p><b>(a) Descriptive</b></p><p>At sunrise, Dickenson Bay is quiet and calm. I can see the sea lying flat and bright, like a sheet of blue glass. I can hear small waves whisper on the sand, and the air smells of salt and fresh johnnycakes from a stall by the road.</p><p><b>(b) Persuasive</b></p><p>Minister, you should put more covered bins on Dickenson Bay beach because litter is spoiling the sand and the sea. Visitors and families deserve a clean beach. Therefore, please send a clean-up team every Sunday before the cruise season begins.</p><p><b>(c)</b> My first paragraph is descriptive because it uses the senses, and my second paragraph is persuasive because it asks the Minister to act.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>Same place, different job. The descriptive paragraph shows the place; the persuasive paragraph asks someone to do something about it. Notice how your words change when your purpose changes.</p>"},
          up: {"title":"One topic, two forms (Challenge)","bloom":"Create","genre":"Argumentative feature article blending four forms, with analytical commentary (25 marks: Content 10, Organization 7, Expression 8)","prompt":"<p>Choose ONE place in the Caribbean that matters to you and that faces a threat (erosion, development, neglect or pollution). Write a short <b>feature article</b> (about 300–400 words) for a regional newspaper whose <b>dominant form is argumentative</b>. It must:</p><ul><li>open with a brief <b>narrative</b> hook;</li><li>include a <b>descriptive</b> passage with at least two sensory images and one figurative technique;</li><li>include an <b>expository</b> paragraph with at least one fact and its source;</li><li>acknowledge and <b>rebut</b> one opposing view;</li><li>end with a specific <b>call to action</b> (who, what, by when).</li></ul><p>Then write a <b>commentary</b> (about 60–90 words) that identifies the dominant form and explains how each subordinate form serves the purpose, using the terms logos, ethos or pathos. Keep the whole response within 500 words.</p>","min":300,"max":500,"frame":"<p><b>Narrative hook:</b> When I was ..., ... Last ..., I ...<br><b>Description:</b> At ..., the ... glows like ... The air smells of ... But ...<br><b>Exposition:</b> The causes are ... According to ..., ...<br><b>Counter-argument and rebuttal:</b> Some argue that ... However, ...<br><b>Call to action:</b> Therefore, I call on ... to ... by ...<br><b>Commentary:</b> The dominant form is argumentative because ... The narrative opening creates pathos by ... The description ... The exposition adds logos ... The rebuttal builds ethos ...</p>","checklist":["My article opens with a short story that leads into the issue.","My description uses two senses and a figurative technique.","My expository paragraph includes a fact with its source.","I acknowledged an opposing view fairly and rebutted it.","My call to action names who, what and by when.","My commentary names the dominant form and explains each subordinate form using logos, ethos or pathos.","I avoided clichés and kept within 500 words."],"rubric":[{"criterion":"Content","marks":10,"look":["remember|grandfather|grandmother|when I was|last (sunday|saturday|year|month|week)|years ago","smell|scent|salt|sound|glitter|glow|cool|warm|roar|whisper|\\blike\\b","\\d|according to|projects|research|report|data","must|should|urge|call on","some argue|critics|opponents|however","dominant","pathos|logos|ethos"],"min":5,"strong":"All four forms are present and the commentary explains how each serves the argument. Next step: make the call to action even more precise.","weak":"Make sure every required element is present: hook, sensory description, sourced fact, rebuttal, call to action, and a commentary using logos, ethos or pathos."},{"criterion":"Organization","marks":7,"look":["however|although|while","therefore|consequently|for this reason|that is why","commentary|dominant"],"min":2,"strong":"The article builds logically from story to scene to evidence to rebuttal to action.","weak":"Order the article so that each form leads into the next, and use transitions such as \"However\" and \"Therefore\"."},{"criterion":"Expression","marks":8,"look":["[.!?]\\s+[A-Z]","\\b(vivid|precise|urgent|evoke|highlight)"],"min":1,"strong":"Fresh, precise word choice and a confident formal register.","weak":"Replace clichés with precise images, vary sentence length, and proofread for agreement and tense."}],"model":"<p><b>Save Grand Anse before the sea takes it</b></p><p>When I was eight, my grandfather walked me to the almond tree at the top of Grand Anse beach in Grenada and told me that, as a boy, he had needed twenty long strides to reach the water from there. Last Sunday I stood under the same tree. The sea was six strides away.</p><p>At sunrise the bay is still beautiful. The water glows like polished turquoise, the sand is cool and fine under bare feet, and the air smells of salt and sea-grape leaves. But at high tide, waves now slap against the roots of the almond tree, and pale scars show where the beach has been eaten away.</p><p>The causes are no mystery. Coral reefs act as natural breakwaters, absorbing most of the energy of incoming waves, yet bleaching and pollution have weakened the reefs that shelter the bay. At the same time, the Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change projects that global sea levels could rise by more than half a metre by 2100. Buildings placed close to the shoreline make matters worse, because they block the natural movement of sand.</p><p>Some argue that new beachfront hotels bring badly needed jobs, and they are right that tourism feeds many Grenadian families. However, a hotel built on a vanishing beach is a poor investment: no visitor flies across the Atlantic to admire a sea wall. Protecting the beach protects the jobs.</p><p>Therefore, I call on the Government of Grenada to enforce a strict building setback along Grand Anse, to fund reef restoration in the bay, and to publish a shoreline management plan within the next twelve months. My grandfather's twenty strides cannot be won back, but our six can still be saved.</p><p><b>Commentary</b></p><p>The dominant form is argumentative, because every part builds towards the call to action. The narrative opening creates pathos by making the loss personal and measurable. The description uses a simile and sensory images to make the beach vivid, so readers feel what is at stake. The exposition adds logos through a cited fact, and the fair rebuttal builds ethos.</p>","teacherTip":"<p>This is how real feature articles work: one dominant purpose, several forms serving it. When you analyse a Paper 2 extract, look for exactly these moves and explain what each one does for the writer.</p>"}
        }
      ]
    }
